"Celer: A Lookup Argument for Large-Scale Queries (https://eprint.iacr.org/2026/1453)\nciteturn4view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://eprint.iacr.org/2026/1453\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 79\nL0: # What a lovely hat\nL1: #### Is it made out of 【0†tin foil†iacr.org】?\nL2: \nL3: 【1†Cryptology ePrint Archive】\nL4: \nL5:   * 【2†Papers】 Updates from the last:\nL6:     * 【3†7 days】\nL7:     * 【4†31 days】\nL8:     * 【5†6 months】\nL9:     * 【6†365 days】\nL10:     * * * *\nL11: \nL12:     * 【7†Listing by year】\nL13:     * 【8†All papers】\nL14:     * 【9†Compact view】\nL15:     * 【10†Subscribe†www.iacr.org】\nL16:     * * * *\nL17: \nL18:     * 【11†How to cite】\nL19:     * * * *\nL20:     * 【12†Harvesting metadata】\nL21:   * 【2†Submissions】\nL22:     * 【13†Submit a paper】\nL23:     * 【14†Revise or withdraw a paper】\nL24:     * 【15†Acceptance and publishing conditions】\nL25: \nL26: 【16†Image: Search Button】\nL27: \nL28: [Input] [Button: Search ]\nL29: #### Paper 2026/1453\nL30: \nL31: ### 【17†Celer: A Lookup Argument for Large-Scale Queries】\nL32: \nL33: Wenjie Qu, National University of Singapore\nL34: \nL35: Yanpei Guo, National University of Singapore\nL36: \nL37: Zhen Xuan, National University of Singapore\nL38: \nL39: Xuanming Liu, Zhejiang University\nL40: \nL41: Jiaheng Zhang, National University of Singapore\nL42: ##### Abstract\nL43: Lookup arguments are designed to prove that all elements in a query vector appear in a lookup table. These arguments are critical for efficiently proving ZK-unfriendly statements, such as range checks and the evaluation of complex functions. Most existing lookup arguments are optimized for scenarios where the query size is smaller than the table size. However, in many real-world applications, the query size $m$ is often much larger than $n$, the table size.\nL44: This disparity leads to inefficiencies in such schemes. To overcome this limitation, we propose Celer, a novel lookup argument in which the prover's runtime increases slowly with $m$, thereby providing improved scalability. The efficiency of our lookup PIOP stems from reducing the number of commitments to be sublinear in the query size $m$, while maintaining field operations linear in $m$. Experimental results demonstrate that our lookup scheme significantly outperforms state-of-the-art lookup schemes.\nL45: For the workload of query size $m=2^{28}$ and table size $n=2^{16}$ (a real world case in proving Llama language model inference), our scheme achieves a 25.9$\\times$ prover-time speedup over plookup and is 9.4$\\times$ faster than LogUp.\nL46: ##### Metadata\nL47: Available format(s)\nL48:     【17†PDF】\nL49: Category\nL50:     【18†Cryptographic protocols】\nL51: Publication info\nL52:     A minor revision of an IACR publication in CRYPTO 2026\nL53: Keywords\nL54:     【19†zkSNARK】【20†lookup argument】\nL55: Contact author(s)\nL56:     wen_jie_qu @ outlook com\nL57: guo yanpei @ u nus edu\nL58: xuanzhen @ u nus edu\nL59: hinsliu @ zju edu cn\nL60: jhzhang @ nus edu sg\nL61: History\nL62:     2026-07-21: last of 2 revisions\nL63:     2026-07-16: received\nL64:     【21†See all versions】\nL65: Short URL\nL66:     【22†https://ia.cr/2026/1453†ia.cr】\nL67: License\nL68: 【23†CC BY†creativecommons.org】\nL69: BibTeX 【24†Image】Copy to clipboard\nL70: \nL71:     @misc{cryptoeprint:2026/1453,\nL72:           author = {Wenjie Qu and Yanpei Guo and Zhen Xuan and Xuanming Liu and Jiaheng Zhang},\nL73:           title = {Celer: A Lookup Argument for Large-Scale Queries},\nL74:           howpublished = {Cryptology {ePrint} Archive, Paper 2026/1453},\nL75:           year = {2026},\nL76:           url = {https://eprint.iacr.org/2026/1453}\nL77:     }\nL78: Note: In order to protect the privacy of readers, eprint.iacr.org does not use cookies or embedded third party content.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nquwenjie.github.io (https://quwenjie.gi